title op6: a gain with no loss is still an attachment

# VR10, and the boundary of the settlement: the one case where the
# word "attachment" survives.
#
# Base holds one file under one name, x.  Onto is quiet.  Off-of adds
# a hard link: the same dnode now also answers to z.  The delta is
# lost NOTHING, gained {z}.
#
# Under the old machinery a gain became an attachment whenever it
# could not be paired with a loss, and there were two ways to get
# there: a genuine new link like this one, and the DEGRADE -- a
# multi-name rename that the pairing step could not read.  The second
# was the defect.  It classified renames as attachments, and an
# attachment carries no antecedent, so realization had nothing to
# look up and the pool materialized.
#
# Set succession deletes the degrade and keeps the genuine case.
# After the settlement an attachment means exactly what its name
# says: the link count grew.  Here nothing was lost, so nothing had
# to be paired, and the pool keeps its dnode because a decided name
# it already holds resolves in pass one -- x is still x.
#
# The fixture is small on purpose.  It is the control that says the
# settlement removed a branch rather than a concept, and it is worth
# having because "no degrade branch" is easy to read as "no
# attachments," which would be wrong and would break every hard link
# anyone adds.
#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  none; no directory changed
#   deltas:          pool 10 lost {}, gained {z}.  An empty lost set
#                    is what makes this an attachment rather than a
#                    rename, and it is the only thing that does
#   decisions:       the pool keeps dnode 10-100 and holds x and z.
#                    Realization resolves in pass one on the name x,
#                    so the lost-set fallback is never reached -- an
#                    empty lost set could not route anywhere anyway
#   conflicts:       none, onto is quiet
#   emission:        one link, hung off x, which the clone already
#                    holds

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared/
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: z

# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: shared
      10-100: x = v0
      10-100: z
